#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068617 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e9590de-9413-5bec-b3f4-315038a3848e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "3a0a00c8-8a86-52de-bc9d-d9219a0735cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-591" :question/problem "![](images/73/7373418f2a0bac416e37662cea5a79101220820aa6f23c50a80fbf6addc5eefd.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=x^2$ and $y=x^5,$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^2$ and $g(x)=x^5.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {x}^{2} & ={x}^{5} \\\\ {x}^{5}-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{3}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/e4/e41991c460504023c4086aa0070904f20d88304aecd42a293c4b0422132b40e0.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=x^5,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=x^2.$\n\nTherefore, the volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}({[\\text{outer}]}^{2}-{[\\text{inner}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[{x}^{5}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}-{x}^{10})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{11})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi }{55}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068618 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7144a491-71ac-565e-9687-3c8ed0a0c0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "a22887cf-cafa-5ba5-9834-e0c435ec86af" :question/math-academy-id "e-592" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd7319762569fbab0e64f03719b41af9dfb45d07aac073a534ea68f842b1ce99.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $x=\\sqrt{4-y^2},$ $x=1,$ and the horizontal lines $y=-1$ and $y=1,$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(y)=\\sqrt{4-y^2}\\,$ and $g(y)=1.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7f/7f875c024b25c37c434a82a4320b4927bde85dc8f0ada3b35837330f829b23c7.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{4-y^2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}]}^{2}-{1}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi (3y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}){|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi (3-\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068645 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04fd7672-4a22-5149-a97f-8910845101b0" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "c8be6b12-aad0-5662-a1ae-fa509f578718" :question/math-academy-id "e-7314" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f6ee4ef4870da694c0ef56cf7395012c9ea1edbe4bc31247d9cdc7b26186b7db.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and $y=x^3,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)=x^3.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ x & ={x}^{6} \\\\ {x}^{6}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{5}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/2a/2abe3a274ab5afee2bc0a85c48c2f1218620b86354c3c4e6e4dd8758ca1af5e5.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=x^3,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}-{[{x}^{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068646 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2e4ab04-30ed-5e74-88e2-e30626b46312" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "de8fda0f-8a0f-5f6e-94ab-3852771288db" :question/math-academy-id "e-7315" :question/problem "![](images/97/97ead66fca615e8f9325d445ca14a8478a276e6dc8d2878a2fd5dbea5d880f7a.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=x^3$ and $x=y,$ where $y \\leq 0,$ as shown above. Find an integral expression for the volume generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Writing our first function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{3}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt[3]{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}$ and $g(y)=y.$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =y \\\\ y & ={y}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $y=0$ and $y=\\pm 1.$ But, since $y \\leq 0,$ the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=-1.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/1a/1ab4ce88964c1a4ce12fdfb93b77810b675bc18e656060629c3544eea47129b2.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt[3]{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[\\sqrt[3]{y}]}^{2}-{[y]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e9590de-9413-5bec-b3f4-315038a3848e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0689e233-9bc4-5d7a-a7c9-a4ddabcf8f8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528319} {:answer/id #uuid "33d5497e-3754-55e7-8f8c-02dbadf4046e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528427} {:answer/id #uuid "6362d776-a3dc-580c-8bc4-2d16e9c2b4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528535} {:answer/id #uuid "a7cc3b11-8155-534b-8103-47d2fb5e7672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528707} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ecc4d4-4427-5ab6-86a2-60f434bcd1e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8ecc4d4-4427-5ab6-86a2-60f434bcd1e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528791} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae92ff64-4bee-5c75-a130-c0515196ac49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5df2e25-639b-5613-82c1-85dbfc89566d" :question/math-academy-id "q-11094" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2bf88537e9a37404dd10dd99769f6933879d07c1ffbb913adc2a4f52b11f48d6.png)\n\nConsider the finite region above bounded by $y=\\sqrt{x}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)={x}^{3}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{x}-{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}(1-{x}^{5/2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/0c/0cdcf559f1498045d3d8cd725593da6adb0459addb1df7cc3c4167b2fd9b6214.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={x}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}-{[{x}^{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{6})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{7}}{7})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{7})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04fd7672-4a22-5149-a97f-8910845101b0" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b824624-e1cb-5f67-82d4-a6a1bb43b515" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{(1-x)}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528451} {:answer/id #uuid "6a378802-e8e4-51be-812e-eb9a47f10ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\text{ }1-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[1-x]}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528554} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa36682-202b-5619-8b29-8025bcc92764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{(1-x)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528670} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9361ee-93d2-5d97-bec7-8dc5e66297ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\text{ }1-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[1-x]}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528769} {:answer/id #uuid "e54a3c9d-8021-57cd-9c71-2255dbcd5636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[1-x]}^{4}-{[\\text{ }1-{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a378802-e8e4-51be-812e-eb9a47f10ca2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\text{ }1-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[1-x]}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528554} :answer-field/id #uuid "c20b2b4b-311d-5118-a601-de5eb214ef9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd1790e6-f31f-5064-a1ab-c0c1da6fc5dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-11096" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4eaf3eac9364aca72506f0d11c7d69332bbf853871d85986e45bd3082a607354.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and $y={x}^{2}-2x+1,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=1-{x}^{2}$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}-2x+1.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 1-{x}^{2} & ={x}^{2}-2x+1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/3f/3fc8d26f99a0b640983b047af4e201fe5f8164c9d6ebc7f5bd234ae706fed796.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={x}^{2}-2x+1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=1-{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[1-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[{x}^{2}-2x+1]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\text{ }1-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[1-x]}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7144a491-71ac-565e-9687-3c8ed0a0c0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6d43040-346c-5a6d-9b66-80aadca8b4ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065526} {:answer/id #uuid "0ad14229-ac80-582b-a455-c69c94e3e805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528327} {:answer/id #uuid "69dba8d8-2b15-5feb-9dd7-9b6a0b0cb0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528552} {:answer/id #uuid "b4406c79-30eb-5ac3-b6ff-9a02bdd4edb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528742} {:answer/id #uuid "c3d0691a-1f69-5347-b891-6fb7ae51a803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3d0691a-1f69-5347-b891-6fb7ae51a803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528777} :answer-field/id #uuid "3475b827-d592-5129-b3d3-ba590aa5b54b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4c43f78a-fa1b-5545-b310-b876b05209de" :question/math-academy-id "q-11100" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by $x=\\sqrt{y}$ and $x={y}^{3}.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $f(y)=\\sqrt{y}$ and $g(y)={y}^{3}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & ={y}^{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{y}-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{y}\\,(1-{y}^{5/2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ and $y=1.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/cd/cdf95299f8b925adcec743141bda4d94ad5542bfa8859fbdf5501ae0d56cd4e8.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)={y}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}-{[{y}^{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{6})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{7}}{7})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{7})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7144a491-71ac-565e-9687-3c8ed0a0c0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31f4d92d-a272-589c-aaa2-26e10752eec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528423} {:answer/id #uuid "4173c919-b0a7-54eb-a3f1-ef3e434be901" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{49\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528468} {:answer/id #uuid "600e3932-6193-582e-b8d3-9f02d1dac288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528526} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4d79fc-2e5f-5cec-b5a4-479e429bcf09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528679} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a21577-4501-5227-9e07-538c3f614cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "600e3932-6193-582e-b8d3-9f02d1dac288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528526} :answer-field/id #uuid "607040d4-42b3-5cc5-ad6f-1ef6c33ab08e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d8dce30-577d-5868-b247-3c35e51af095" :question/math-academy-id "q-11102" :question/problem "![](images/62/6250d388875796ad05e4d3eb8a5f625b6a633cbf0e9efb24926f72a9c6ceac7f.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and $x=1-\\sqrt{y},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Writing our first function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)=\\sqrt{1-y}\\,$ and $g(y)=1-\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\sqrt{1-y} & =1-\\sqrt{y} \\\\ 1-y & =1-2\\sqrt{y}+y \\\\ 2y-2\\sqrt{y} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{y}(\\sqrt{y}-1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/b6/b630edf4ad0b61defcb7aba67902f75202a8f6e9a209384fb28650c06545113f.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=1-\\sqrt{y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=\\sqrt{1-y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{1-y}]}^{2}-{[1-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}([1-y]-[1-2\\sqrt{y}+y])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2\\sqrt{y}-2y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{1/2}-y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {(\\frac{2{y}^{3/2}}{3}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\pi [(\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{1}{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7144a491-71ac-565e-9687-3c8ed0a0c0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2531d59f-f2ae-5972-ad8d-0b5fd54daec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528397} {:answer/id #uuid "abfcd8a6-cfe4-579a-ae03-ab736d027afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528718} {:answer/id #uuid "beed519c-f20b-5284-af42-9fbc05bb7cb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528766} {:answer/id #uuid "cd6260e8-7e2e-517a-94a3-c2333c4374dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528804} {:answer/id #uuid "e25642d2-500b-5440-8219-49e59a725b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd6260e8-7e2e-517a-94a3-c2333c4374dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528804} :answer-field/id #uuid "68c55f83-abc0-585e-b0d0-0f5142052ec5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4870784d-cfa2-5882-86d4-3e6f72784ac7" :question/math-academy-id "q-11107" :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f94662dcae88371bb47d9748d002e38c6e521b0ba689e31e782ce288d266a58.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2$ and ${x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=1,$ where $x\\ge 0,$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Given that $x\\ge 0,$ writing our functions in terms of $y,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=2\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{2+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=1\\, & ⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2+{y}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2+{y}^{2} & =1+2{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$    \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/41/4177abe03f95230a3176738cddbc556d2e4c3703fe72c1391a53c1ecb5f82181.png)\n\n  \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=\\sqrt{2+{y}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[\\sqrt{2+{y}^{2}}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}((2+{y}^{2})-(1+2{y}^{2}))\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3})|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(1-\\frac{1}{3})-((-1)+\\frac{1}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04fd7672-4a22-5149-a97f-8910845101b0" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5843c213-01a6-5e2b-9bc8-4fd5d86dbaad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}({e}^{{x}^{2}}-{e}^{2-x})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528509} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9f8c59-1dc9-5e0d-8c2f-687618e26ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}({e}^{2-x}-{e}^{{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528513} {:answer/id #uuid "8627ef67-ddb2-57f6-bf5f-dfecb65a8067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}({e}^{4-2x}-{e}^{2{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528618} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ef96a4-129c-5753-ac25-dc9fa916032a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({e}^{2{x}^{2}}-{e}^{4-2x})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528704} {:answer/id #uuid "d14478ca-4070-5ce9-90a8-172b823c4e8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({e}^{4-2x}-{e}^{2{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528816}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8627ef67-ddb2-57f6-bf5f-dfecb65a8067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}({e}^{4-2x}-{e}^{2{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528618} :answer-field/id #uuid "114213cb-ff5a-5f09-8a9a-c11e6cbf3545" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8f211adc-046d-5c01-ba2b-26f9e0e2be96" :question/math-academy-id "q-122004" :question/problem "![](images/9c/9c5d2e2426a817b62f8cd834d7d31786c5f2dfd52a7b784fd0bf337abed6c4fc.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $y={e}^{2-x}$ and $y={e}^{{x}^{2}},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={e}^{2-x}$ and $g(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {e}^{2-x} & ={e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2-x & ={x}^{2} \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+x-2 \\\\ (x+2)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-2$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/8a/8a7f49165698bdac791a31a058c3228e6e2beab9f5ee9393971aa0e9aecd27b8.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={e}^{{x}^{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)={e}^{2-x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}({[{e}^{2-x}]}^{2}-{[{e}^{{x}^{2}}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}({e}^{4-2x}-{e}^{2{x}^{2}})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529095 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e9590de-9413-5bec-b3f4-315038a3848e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48706475-0664-565e-bd2d-7f83441bc4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528481} {:answer/id #uuid "99d1f1a0-32aa-5964-8ea7-584cb8f97f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528665} {:answer/id #uuid "c448d435-9bcd-595a-9b15-481488adc3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528780} {:answer/id #uuid "c948edf4-7c1a-595a-9006-e62d1d1f4937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528793} {:answer/id #uuid "f7aa2104-1ab4-5d6d-8862-d71d4c61233f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c448d435-9bcd-595a-9b15-481488adc3cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528780} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c25b804-adcd-5109-97e9-a671522d2fa7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17abd4d5-764f-50c1-82c3-4fcec4b8a574" :question/math-academy-id "q-122546" :question/problem "![](images/8c/8ce8a8061ec087c39ce93d73d0566832f33c4e6ce5c2f5185170c9372a0d88fa.png)\n\nConsider the finite region above bounded by $y=2x+2$ and $y=\\sqrt{4x+4},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x\\text{-axis.}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2x+2$ and $g(x)=\\sqrt{4x+4}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2x+2 & =\\sqrt{4x+4} \\\\ 2(x+1) & =\\sqrt{4x+4} \\\\ 4(x+1)^{2} & =4x+4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1 & =x+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x & =0 \\\\ x(x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-1$ to $x=0.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/0a/0a6bdcc071500ac8dd40143c3219a14c80fc73d543d7307cb2c880a22db793a3.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=f(x)=2x+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=g(x)=\\sqrt{4x+4}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[\\sqrt{4x+4}]}^{2}-{[2x+2]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[4x+4-(2(x+1))^{2}]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[4(x+1)-4({x}^{2}+2x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}[x+1-({x}^{2}+2x+1)]\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{x}^{2}-x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {(-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2})|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =4\\pi (0-(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2e4ab04-30ed-5e74-88e2-e30626b46312" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47a3aa53-5278-571f-be2f-61617323e3e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}({y}^{2}-9)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528479} {:answer/id #uuid "5ade3240-071a-5254-a5f2-d65b724dcda1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(9-{y}^{2})(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528515} {:answer/id #uuid "8754fbd1-e952-53e6-8efa-f251620f9bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(y-3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528622} {:answer/id #uuid "96993872-6660-5370-b129-947f15c62c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}({y}^{2}-9)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528656} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa114cb-eaa2-5372-8f8c-a3f2840e8740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{3}(9-{y}^{2})(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528812}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ade3240-071a-5254-a5f2-d65b724dcda1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(9-{y}^{2})(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528515} :answer-field/id #uuid "c008af03-a6bf-532f-9d1b-deae201a2d39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d1bfa1c-c252-5832-a4ec-b38acfcd7492" :question/math-academy-id "q-122558" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9f1ea82756eed4a815afb54defd8425631a57a0bcd1dfaba2dbf5b24b43b518.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $x=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ and $x=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ and $g(y)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0 \\\\ (y-3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=1$ and $y=3.$ \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/54/54278318429e01bc3fa2c0325fea7c7a918aacf096b1510d8acc87d3ffc53a73.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=f(y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=g(y)=3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}({[3\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2}-{[y\\operatorname{ln}⁡y]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(9(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2}-{y}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}(9-{y}^{2})(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529176 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2e4ab04-30ed-5e74-88e2-e30626b46312" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10a507d3-b82b-5812-b4bd-1a60a94b11a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}({[\\frac{1}{2}y-1]}^{2}-y+2)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528337} {:answer/id #uuid "3531e655-6ae7-5b2d-ab95-2fc419eb17a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}({[\\frac{1}{2}y-1]}^{2}-y+2)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528432} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c80ec3-99ea-519d-b6ba-11c8b1be9972" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(\\sqrt{y-2}-\\frac{1}{2}y+1)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528733} {:answer/id #uuid "b6ea001f-721c-5acf-821a-a0ad541670bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(y-2-{[\\frac{1}{2}y-1]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528745} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a469bb-c186-589c-8768-0ebb679178a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}(y-2-{[\\frac{1}{2}y-1]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6ea001f-721c-5acf-821a-a0ad541670bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(y-2-{[\\frac{1}{2}y-1]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528745} :answer-field/id #uuid "82f32dfc-9143-5166-b02d-1b46261da802" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d84428ea-0ba4-5227-96d6-43b34422e545" :question/math-academy-id "q-123365" :question/problem "![](images/55/55308256c72aaf122c04c0b7f3a987fccd72d2513dd4edb6cf0ea4ce857522e5.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y=2x+2$ and $x=\\sqrt{y-2},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our first function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x+2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}y-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)=\\frac{1}{2}y-1$ and $g(y)=\\sqrt{y-2}.$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}y-1 & =\\sqrt{y-2} \\\\ y-2 & =2\\sqrt{y-2} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(y-2) \\\\ (y-2)^{2}-4(y-2) & =0 \\\\ (y-2)((y-2)-4) & =0 \\\\ (y-2)(y-6) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=2$ to $y=6.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/8e/8e621715ded3c5ed527d2367785707939b1e3593707eaafe0b4c5e4509883e49.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=f(y)=\\frac{1}{2}y-1,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=g(y)=\\sqrt{y-2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}({[\\sqrt{y-2}]}^{2}-{[\\frac{1}{2}y-1]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{6}(y-2-{[\\frac{1}{2}y-1]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2e4ab04-30ed-5e74-88e2-e30626b46312" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a6213c7-b58b-555d-8665-b22f7434f0cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({e}^{y+2}-{e}^{{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528448} {:answer/id #uuid "75579b0b-8d22-5de4-92bc-15ad6764edee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({e}^{2y+4}-{e}^{2{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528569} {:answer/id #uuid "8fe77835-b4df-5c45-a282-5fb5cd15ea31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({e}^{{y}^{2}}-{e}^{y+2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528646} {:answer/id #uuid "bc0fb309-ab27-5ba5-ae7f-f3a42d9ac2c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{1}({e}^{2y+4}-{e}^{2{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528758} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ef0c42-44f4-5b64-9ae2-1f685ea0e6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({e}^{2{y}^{2}}-{e}^{2y+4})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75579b0b-8d22-5de4-92bc-15ad6764edee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({e}^{2y+4}-{e}^{2{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528569} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d70164c-5d29-5881-aced-1f4582314e1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bef34f9-b7a4-5afd-919c-8a8bc54bd07d" :question/math-academy-id "q-123369" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb3afbc1bebf1abc77b0495135e02b26da362ffb346aa261d42ba43dca94ec66.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $x={e}^{{y}^{2}}$ and $x={e}^{y+2},$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(y)={e}^{{y}^{2}}$ and $g(y)={e}^{y+2}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {e}^{{y}^{2}} & ={e}^{y+2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =y+2 \\\\ {y}^{2}-y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y+1)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-1$ and $y=2.$ \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/b6/b6db4894c0cf0204ae676a0350771cf47359031f30fb975d48d5864b1592e673.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=f(y)={e}^{{y}^{2}},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=g(y)={e}^{y+2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[{e}^{y+2}]}^{2}-{[{e}^{{y}^{2}}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({e}^{2y+4}-{e}^{2{y}^{2}})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7144a491-71ac-565e-9687-3c8ed0a0c0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29fab220-23bc-5366-b741-676d8657ce65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528405} {:answer/id #uuid "8cfc9f8a-4976-5927-99bf-331c5811136e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528637} {:answer/id #uuid "b80dc7eb-9456-5e23-b6c7-2d2f2b7e9338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528748} {:answer/id #uuid "c15e3f58-b077-5947-b270-fa6badb4c890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528775} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e6f5d2-255a-53e4-a0a8-a54da7d2533b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b80dc7eb-9456-5e23-b6c7-2d2f2b7e9338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528748} :answer-field/id #uuid "f62f58ca-a3ef-528b-83c7-bf2ccd3c2d04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10f20a34-5dca-5f03-b661-36330ae0c9f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-123488" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2888d7fef98dacad56792a627ef1554ed28def615f1431de35d3ba9820fe0de.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $x={y}^{2}+2$ and $y=2-x,$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Writing our second function in terms of $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ny=2-x\\,⟹\\,x=2-y.\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)={y}^{2}+2$ and $g(y)=2-y.$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{2}+2 & =2-y \\\\ {y}^{2}+y & =0 \\\\ y(y+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=0.$    \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/af/afe7bb6fbc5f523e04bdd8c5bc9e899af356729da0e4c7822bd7619dfeb3cb95.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=f(y)={y}^{2}+2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=g(y)=2-y.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({[2-y]}^{2}-{[{y}^{2}+2]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-4y+{y}^{2}-({y}^{4}+4{y}^{2}+4))\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}(-{y}^{4}-3{y}^{2}-4y)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-{y}^{3}-2{y}^{2})|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =\\pi [0-(\\frac{1}{5}+1-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e9590de-9413-5bec-b3f4-315038a3848e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06f41b0c-2317-5c10-90c1-9b4de8bc9b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528320} {:answer/id #uuid "305b1923-496e-5c90-8b84-1fed6def62da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528420} {:answer/id #uuid "60ebdefb-f4b8-5761-88be-e25296f92b37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528529} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dc5a4a-b487-510c-9286-d6baaafc6cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528696} {:answer/id #uuid "ff22f09a-b5a4-56d1-8ab3-09ffe4f90eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "06f41b0c-2317-5c10-90c1-9b4de8bc9b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528320} :answer-field/id #uuid "49c6f063-8a64-5664-9b82-3dbd283261da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1f7cc0c-8da5-5f3b-9d2b-4e0b2b3836e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-16879" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by $y=2\\sqrt{x}$ and $y=2x.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}$ and $g(x)=2x.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2\\sqrt{x} & =2x \\\\ x-\\sqrt{x} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}(\\sqrt{x}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ and $x=1.$ \n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/d0/d0b8efe1137ecce643a16b690ba1b05f72a8f7f1e90a56e0b6c379d95886f321.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=2x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}-{[2x]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x-4{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(2{x}^{2}-4\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(2-\\frac{4}{3})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e9590de-9413-5bec-b3f4-315038a3848e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0682a3b0-8757-5407-94ef-a6f4c17bf7bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528318} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd79221-ded8-58df-aad5-c5b97ded34f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{28}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528329} {:answer/id #uuid "17ab5395-aeaa-506c-9157-a7c38f8d6142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528361} {:answer/id #uuid "3952d716-6c08-5913-94b8-82d74fa976a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{216\\pi }{73}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528446} {:answer/id #uuid "8056047e-a697-5657-982e-4344939eaf56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{18\\pi }{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0682a3b0-8757-5407-94ef-a6f4c17bf7bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528318} :answer-field/id #uuid "7621ba02-b92d-5a3e-a125-55f5917fa01d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a105b226-a38b-52d5-a632-e4ef4cd97215" :question/math-academy-id "q-3384" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f27cf1fddfbbbdb9dd2593567743494ae15e061bca8d1e111ac1f1561561943.png)\n\nConsider the finite region above bounded by $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $g(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\sqrt{x} & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{x}({x}^{3/2}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/bf/bf8d9039cbaa7fe5559d8d9d036063739e572b2abc46741668a88c09d9bcd28a.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=f(x)={x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=g(x)=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[\\sqrt{x}]}^{2}-{[{x}^{2}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(x-{x}^{4})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{5})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529132 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068775 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e9590de-9413-5bec-b3f4-315038a3848e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c77480c4-6dc8-5251-b1f4-72672af1b05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{500\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c77480c4-6dc8-5251-b1f4-72672af1b05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{500\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528786} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c19b998-512f-50eb-99a5-8be35004295f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529010}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61a0b761-b748-5011-958d-51f405f814ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-362200" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by $y=x+6$ and $y={x}^{2}.$ Find the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x+6$ and $g(x)={x}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ x+6 & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-6 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-2$ to $x=3.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/dd/dd8dc332f56d3b851647e5ed0fa8208eb5d3daf6e704a42ab5cffdb78ed78585.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={x}^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=x+6.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{3}((x+6)^{2}-({x}^{2})^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{3}({x}^{2}+12x+36-{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+6{x}^{2}+36x-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})\\,{|}_{-2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{27}{3}+54+108-\\frac{243}{5})-(-\\frac{8}{3}+24-72+\\frac{32}{5})] \\\\  & =\\frac{500\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2e4ab04-30ed-5e74-88e2-e30626b46312" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd9b3f4-ffc7-5550-9361-297d53cecc63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(1-y)\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528377} {:answer/id #uuid "3453a2dc-525b-5c37-b554-085bee2c19a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}-[\\sqrt{1-y}])\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528430} {:answer/id #uuid "6b5329f2-5e6d-5dfd-ac89-e404f175543d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{1-y}]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528558} {:answer/id #uuid "868edb5d-0086-5d04-b4be-5d14df3a0c84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528621} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b63ff9-b2d6-5c35-a869-8a8fe63e7eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}([2-\\sqrt{y}]-{[\\sqrt{1-y}]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b5329f2-5e6d-5dfd-ac89-e404f175543d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{1-y}]}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528558} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1f86725-a9a0-5a34-a3e3-7d5e69393d36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f666ceb4-bd3b-5b00-9ee5-dd4ca3fe5f6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-50225" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9b328ecd6017922e429f11c57aca0ff452e4dc6a1476b14a448c6c9edd6b6679.png)\n\nConsider the region bounded by $x=2-\\sqrt{y}$ and $y=1-{x}^{2}$  from $y=0$ to $y=1,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated ${360}^{∘}$ about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our second function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=1-{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $f(y)=2-\\sqrt{y}$ and $g(y)=\\sqrt{1-y}.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/7d/7d92b9f69ab27a2620ae94bfb4937509fc9db6dafdec4384af5de33a4dfecd30.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=\\sqrt{1-y},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)=2-\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{1-y}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[2-\\sqrt{y}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{1-y}]}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04fd7672-4a22-5149-a97f-8910845101b0" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "997c8b34-acb8-5d51-b0ae-736b81777bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{6})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528664} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd2a9c4-8368-5788-ae30-4f5d3035458d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{(x-{x}^{3})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528799} {:answer/id #uuid "cdeb4b38-6351-5631-8b83-3929e2d6da84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{6})\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528806} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e5c7c0-9172-51b5-89ba-14cbe12807a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528819} {:answer/id #uuid "eac52d03-8f6a-50d0-8ebf-326eb11bfb8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{(x-{x}^{3})}^{2}\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "997c8b34-acb8-5d51-b0ae-736b81777bc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{6})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528664} :answer-field/id #uuid "f148f577-f7bb-5169-8125-f796d9a90542" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abb34aa3-eece-5767-8c0d-162486a46e00" :question/math-academy-id "q-50227" :question/problem "![](images/37/37bf4ff4ad6eb63c9bf51bc5fd188ddc0f5bd58bfd4a097617f56fb5de151300.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by $y={x}^{3}$ and $y=x$ where $x\\ge 0,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)={x}^{3}$ and $g(x)=x.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ {x}^{3} & =x \\\\ {x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $x=0$ and $x=\\pm 1.$ But, since $x\\ge 0,$ the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=1.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/f8/f8850008be348bd78f48837def8691d3a4155e70d13909369c70da2eb31fffb4.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=f(x)={x}^{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=g(x)=x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({[x]}^{2}-{[{x}^{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}-{x}^{6})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529088 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2e4ab04-30ed-5e74-88e2-e30626b46312" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b562af0-779e-564f-bf8e-eff2bb21be8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{(2{y}^{2}-\\sqrt{3})}^{2}\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528590} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0882aa-5d49-5ef5-b6bb-ff8a59b2a3b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(5-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528640} {:answer/id #uuid "b327338b-bdbd-5feb-84e7-2e98c88c2c12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(\\sqrt{3}-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528740} {:answer/id #uuid "b77d9887-e116-582a-9a16-739bb87b78c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(8-3{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528746} {:answer/id #uuid "bc2f2b0c-e5aa-5387-92f6-9f8ca058ee33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528759}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc2f2b0c-e5aa-5387-92f6-9f8ca058ee33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528759} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d83603e-459b-5049-90d7-a03f49d1df85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b6da66a-d01e-54fd-b6f1-7c1c4b4f3048" :question/math-academy-id "q-50239" :question/problem "![](images/96/96f465c4da3f2e482fa0224e23564d27e363a727602ec05ed6daad8213807211.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=5$ and $x=\\sqrt{3},$ where $x\\ge 0,$ as shown above. Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "Writing our first function in terms of $y,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}=5\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{5-2{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{5-2{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3 & =5-2{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-1$ to $y=1.$ \n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/54/54fbb219933d282480f70d8f87cbb8225c126979c58a5be6faed0a98eaf97c51.png)\n\n \n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=\\sqrt{5-2{y}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}({[\\sqrt{5-2{y}^{2}}]}^{2}-{[\\sqrt{3}]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}([5-2{y}^{2}]-3)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{1}(2-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04fd7672-4a22-5149-a97f-8910845101b0" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75946804-66ca-5423-99e7-7c2f6c1fe690" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-{x}^{6})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528571} {:answer/id #uuid "81d15c1d-e7f1-54d0-a40c-3b475025c5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(64{x}^{2}-{x}^{8})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528610} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0118b8-bbf7-5150-a7c4-acdba0b36009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(4{x}^{2}-{x}^{8})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528682} {:answer/id #uuid "b0a5d8da-aae6-5343-924a-f1c245defe94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(64{x}^{3}-{x}^{9})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528729} {:answer/id #uuid "cedcb368-116f-5423-9a88-50b3d7afd4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}(64{x}^{2}-{x}^{8})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81d15c1d-e7f1-54d0-a40c-3b475025c5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(64{x}^{2}-{x}^{8})\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528610} :answer-field/id #uuid "14fbe87c-693f-54c6-b758-8a05c9ef6642" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de3cc4c9-f0a7-5d21-89e4-4f5e7276aeec" :question/math-academy-id "q-86111" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by $y=8x$ and $y={x}^{4}.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=8x$ and $g(x)={x}^{4}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 8x & ={x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{4}-8x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{3}-8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=0$ to $x=2.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/ac/ac3248f7ada22b91c42971a0a85692625321cafa82ced12215671435ab584d00.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)={x}^{4},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=8x.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({[8x]}^{2}-{[{x}^{4}]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}(64{x}^{2}-{x}^{8})\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068802 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04fd7672-4a22-5149-a97f-8910845101b0" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Integral Representing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the X-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce5f341-ccad-58e7-a3c7-e873e1465618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}+4x+12)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528331} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6907c7-0e08-5c4c-ab9a-a8400cdb535f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}+4x+12)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528378} {:answer/id #uuid "5b71884b-5002-5915-86da-70993ec5a763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({x}^{4}-4{x}^{2}+12x-1)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528516} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1f70b4-5f05-5a8a-abb1-ca42c8b73e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}+12)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528723} {:answer/id #uuid "bda440f4-5406-5b24-ab61-10164ddee7b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{0}({x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}+2)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528762}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c6907c7-0e08-5c4c-ab9a-a8400cdb535f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}+4x+12)\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528378} :answer-field/id #uuid "133f90f1-eeb0-5270-b7c6-2666bad917ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89b9341d-d36f-5db4-a196-956e022f6dd9" :question/math-academy-id "q-86118" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by $y=2-x$ and $y=4-{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following gives the volume of the solid generated when that region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2-x$ and $g(x)=4-{x}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x) \\\\ 2-x & =4-{x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-x-2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $x=-1$ to $x=2.$\n\nLet's draw our vertical arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing *away* from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/ce/ce74cc02284807691e00fc59d00240520d392cdc01b8efafcd66518d717a607e.png)\n\nThe vertical arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $y=g(x)=2-x,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $y=f(x)=4-{x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({[4-{x}^{2}]}^{2}-{[2-x]}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-1}^{2}({x}^{4}-9{x}^{2}+4x+12)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068776 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7144a491-71ac-565e-9687-3c8ed0a0c0a9" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Solid Generated by Rotation About the Y-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53b1658a-0743-5f39-aa3b-81a36c8036ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{84\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528502} {:answer/id #uuid "78780af1-b667-5db4-80fc-158b6cdd4cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{384\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528580} {:answer/id #uuid "a22fb4d8-f9d7-52f8-ba8d-91050811627a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{34\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528691} {:answer/id #uuid "ac201446-7ce5-558f-be28-299a3a97fe4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{38\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528719} {:answer/id #uuid "d3652865-5e1f-5c8f-897b-2f29a227539f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78780af1-b667-5db4-80fc-158b6cdd4cf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{384\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186528580} :answer-field/id #uuid "bac40885-b168-57ac-9764-80cef2e04f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bb5bb053-af9b-561d-bd9f-9db0ee360eaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-86145" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by $x={y}^{2}-6$ and $x=-2.$ Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Let $f(y)={y}^{2}-6$ and $g(y)=-2.$\n\nFirst, we determine the limits of integration. To do that, we find the intersections of the curves by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ {y}^{2}-6 & =-2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the limits of integration are from $y=-2$ and $y=2.$\n\nLet's draw our horizontal arrow through the region to determine the inner function and the outer function. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](images/ba/ba9886ace429eee313ac208f7a8aebd93993c734e4382656dcf31ef15d04cd00.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner function $x=g(y)=-2,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer function $x=f(y)={y}^{2}-6.$\n\nTherefore, the integral that gives our volume is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}([\\text{outer}]^{2}-[\\text{inner}]^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}({[{y}^{2}-6]}^{2}-{[-2]}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{-2}^{2}({y}^{4}-12{y}^{2}+32)\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-4{y}^{3}+32y)|}_{-2}^{2} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{32}{5}-32+64)-(-\\frac{32}{5}+32-64)] \\\\  & =\\frac{384\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
